## Third-party data attribution

### EMNIST dataset
Cohen, G., Afshar, S., Tapson, J., & van Schaik, A. (2017).
EMNIST: an extension of MNIST to handwritten letters.
http://arxiv.org/abs/1702.05373

EMNIST is derived from NIST Special Database 19. Public domain dataset.

### MNIST dataset
LeCun, Y., Cortes, C., & Burges, C. (1998). The MNIST database of handwritten
digits. http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/

### USPS dataset
Hull, J. J. (1994). A database for handwritten text recognition research.
IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence.

### TTS audio — NOT covered by the MIT license above

The following MP3 files were generated with the `edge-tts` Python package,
which calls the online neural-voice service behind Microsoft Edge's Read Aloud:

- `audio/tts-zh-tw/` (zh-TW-YunJheNeural; the `young-aria/`, `warm-jenny/` and
  `calm-guy/` subfolders use zh-TW-HsiaoChenNeural, zh-TW-HsiaoYuNeural and
  zh-TW-YunJheNeural)
- `audio/tts-zh-tw-hsiaochen/` (zh-TW-HsiaoChenNeural)
- `audio/tts-en/` (en-US-AriaNeural, en-US-JennyNeural, en-US-GuyNeural)
- the 21 English clips (`*_en*`) in `audio/tts-zh-tw-voxcpm/` (en-US-AriaNeural)

Microsoft has not published terms that grant a license to use or redistribute
this output. Its Terms of Use state: "Unless otherwise specified, the Services
are for your personal and non-commercial use." The repo author holds no rights
in these recordings and cannot license them, so the MIT license does not apply
to them. Reuse them at your own risk. For commercial use or public
redistribution, regenerate the audio with a service or model whose terms grant
rights to its output.

The 169 Chinese clips in `audio/tts-zh-tw-voxcpm/` were generated with VoxCPM2
from a Common Voice zh-TW speaker; see README.md.

(Checked 2026-11-08. Earlier versions said "Free to use per Microsoft Edge
browser ToS". That statement had no source and is withdrawn.)
